==Introduction==

===Abstract===

The Confidential Assets extension of Confidential Transactions (CT) on Liquid hides the amount and asset of blinded outputs from a third-party observer.
However, the legitimate recipient of the output still learns this information through some form of encryption protocol: the sender performs an Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman key exchange (ECDH) between its ephemeral key and the recipient's blinding key, the shared secret is then used to derive the output's range proof seed, which in turn piggybacks the encrypted amount and asset.
An attacker with a cryptographically relevant quantum computer (CRQC) can solve the discrete logarithm problem and thus break ECDH with Shor's algorithm.
Given both public keys, they can retroactively recover the amount and asset of confidential outputs recorded today, threatening users' privacy.
New proposal of [[elip-0222.mediawiki|Hybrid Key Encapsulation]] (HKE) replaces this ECDH with a key encapsulation that combines it with the post-quantum KEM <code>ML-KEM-768</code>.
This document specifies an application of the proposed HKE for safeguarding the privacy of outputs on Liquid: new HKE outputs, the corresponding consensus rules, changes of transaction structure, and new confidential address format.

