===Motivation===

Today a sender paying a confidential address picks an ephemeral key <code>e</code>, publishes <code>E = e*G</code> in the output's <code>nNonce</code> field, and derives a range-proof seed from the ECDH of <code>e</code> and the recipient's blinding key <code>B</code>.
The recipient recomputes the seed from <code>E</code> and its blinding private key <code>b</code>, rewinds the range proof, and recovers the amount, the asset and both blinding factors.

An adversary with a cryptographically relevant quantum computer (CRQC) can therefore compute the seed of any output with an exposed blinding key <code>B</code>, including outputs recorded long before the CRQC existed (''harvest-now-decrypt-later'' attack).

The [[elip-0222.mediawiki|HKE]] protocol protects the range-proof seed against this attack by replacing the classical key exchange with a hybrid quantum-resistant key encapsulation.
This document specifies HKE deployment on Liquid.
Specifically, it introduces new ''HKE outputs'' and ''HKE confidential addresses'' along with new consensus rules and wallet requirements accompanying them.
We note that this proposal protects only the ''confidentiality'' of outputs against quantum attackers.
It does not address two other attacks that a CRQC enables: inflation, since a CRQC breaks the binding of Pedersen commitments, and individual theft, since it can forge the ECDSA and Schnorr signatures that authorize UTXOs spending.

==Description==

A payment to an HKE confidential address proceeds as follows:

# The recipient derives <code>msk</code> for its scriptPubKey from its master blinding key <code>mbk</code>, and publishes the scriptPubKey with the HKE public key <code>(B_bytes, ek)</code> as an HKE confidential address: <code>B_bytes</code> is the compressed blinding public key and <code>ek</code> is the ML-KEM-768 public encapsulation key.
# The sender decodes the address, runs <code>HKE.Encaps(B_bytes, ek)</code> to obtain <code>K</code> and the ciphertext <code>(E_bytes, c)</code>, uses <code>K</code> as the seed of the output's range proof, and writes the ciphertext into the output's <code>nNonce</code> using the encoding below. <code>E_bytes</code> is the sender's compressed ephemeral public key and <code>c</code> is the ML-KEM-768 ciphertext.
# The recipient finds the output by its scriptPubKey, extracts <code>(E_bytes, c)</code> from <code>nNonce</code>, computes <code>K := HKE.Decaps((E_bytes, c), msk)</code> with the <code>msk</code> of that scriptPubKey, and rewinds the range proof with the seed <code>K</code> to recover the amount, the asset and both blinding factors.

The range-proof logic does not change: <code>secp256k1_rangeproof_sign</code> and <code>secp256k1_rangeproof_rewind</code> ([https://github.com/BlockstreamResearch/secp256k1-zkp secp256k1-zkp]) take the 32-byte seed as their <code>nonce</code> argument for classical and HKE outputs alike.
Outputs with a classical nonce remain valid and keep the classical seed derivation.
Range proofs in issuances, for the issued amount and for the reissuance tokens, are unaffected: their seed is a blinding private key of the issuer itself, whose public key the transaction does not contain, so there is no key exchange to harvest.

