Figures assume a P2WPKH output with Elements' default 52-bit range proof (4174 B) and a single-input surjection proof (67 B), and ignore transaction overhead.

The proposed discount preserves the fee treatment of classical confidential outputs, while a block holds about half as many HKE outputs.
Discounted CT already separates fees from block space for range and surjection proofs; the unchanged block and standard-transaction limits still bound physical size.

The discount on the nonce applies to every output, including one with an explicit value, which has no range proof to seed.
Today this lets an explicit output carry the 32 bytes of a classical nonce at no fee; an HKE nonce would raise this to 1120 bytes.
This document therefore rejects an HKE nonce on an output with an explicit value ([[#output-validity|Output validity]]), so that every HKE nonce belongs to a confidetial output.

<span id="address-encoding"></span>
===Address encoding===

Today's confidential addresses use <code>blech32</code> (witness version 0), <code>blech32m</code> (version 1 and above), or Base58Check for P2PKH and P2SH; none defines a field for the ~1.2 KB encapsulation key.
A new address type with its own HRPs makes parsers that have not upgraded reject HKE confidential addresses.
The payload keeps the fields of today's confidential segwit address, <code>witver</code>, <code>B_bytes</code> and <code>program</code>, and adds <code>hke_version</code> and <code>ek</code>.

P2PKH and P2SH, which have Base58 confidential addresses today, are deliberately not supported.
A wallet that adopts HKE confidential addresses needs new code anyway and can move its users to a native segwit script at the same time.
Witness versions 1 to 16 accept any program length allowed by BIP141 (2 to 40 bytes), leaving room for future witness programs without another address format.

The address uses the bech32 alphabet but is not <code>bech32</code>, <code>bech32m</code> or <code>blech32m</code>: it replaces their BCH checksum with CRC-32.
The guarantees of <code>blech32</code>'s checksum do not extend to this length: its generator has period 1023, so some pairs of character errors 1023 positions apart go undetected.
CRC-32 provides a 32-bit check for accidental corruption, with an undetected-error probability of 2<sup>-32</sup> for uniformly random checksum differences.
Its usual 32-bit burst guarantee does not apply to this serialization: reflected CRC computation, big-endian checksum bytes and MSB-first base32 permit shorter undetected bursts across the payload/checksum boundary.
The reference tests include such a collision; the checksum does not protect against deliberate address substitution.

For <code>ML-KEM-768</code>, the payload is 1239 bytes and the mainnet or regtest address 1993 characters for P2WPKH, and 1251 bytes and 2012 characters for P2WSH or P2TR; testnet adds one character.
The bech32 alphabet in uppercase fits QR alphanumeric mode, in which each of these addresses needs a version-27 QR code at error-correction level L.
HKE confidential addresses are meant to be scanned or copied, not typed.
The size of <code>ek</code> dominates the address length, which is why it is the main criterion for KEM selection ([[elip-0222.mediawiki#rationale|<code>elip-0222.mediawiki</code>, Rationale]]).

<span id="separate-key-derivation"></span>
===Separate key derivation===

<code>msk</code> is derived under an HMAC key other than <code>mbk</code>, so it is independent of the classical blinding key of the same scriptPubKey.
This matters because a classical blinding key gives no protection against a CRQC: its public key appears in every classical confidential address of the scriptPubKey, and a CRQC recovers the private key from it.
If <code>msk</code> were equal to that private key, the CRQC would also learn <code>dk</code> and could decapsulate every HKE output to the address.
Deriving <code>msk</code> from <code>mbk</code> rather than from a new secret requires no backup beyond <code>mbk</code>, and lets existing watch-only setups that hold <code>mbk</code> follow HKE outputs.

<span id="simplicity-nonce-hashing"></span>
===Simplicity nonce hashing===

<code>c</code> enters Simplicity's hashes as <code>SHA256(c)</code> rather than in full, following the convention Simplicity already uses for variable-length output data: the scriptPubKey, the range proof and the surjection proof also enter as their SHA-256.
Each nonce's contribution to these hashes stays self-delimiting by its prefix: 1 byte for <code>0x00</code>, 33 bytes for <code>0x01</code> to <code>0x03</code>, and 65 bytes for <code>0x04</code> and <code>0x05</code>.
The 65-byte hash input is distinct from the 1121-byte transaction encoding, which carries the full ciphertext.

Classical nonce hashing remains unchanged; compatibility of existing program commitments must be established by the amended Simplicity specification.
A program that rebuilds an output's hash from <code>output_nonce</code> and <code>nonce_hash</code> obtains the classical encoding for an HKE output, so its comparison fails rather than accepting a modified <code>c</code>.

==Backwards Compatibility==

Nodes that have not upgraded cannot deserialize an HKE nonce and reject any transaction or block that contains one; see [[#activation|Activation]].

After activation:

* '''Classical and HKE outputs coexist.''' An output with a classical nonce is recognized by its prefix, and wallets recover it with the classical seed derivation. HKE cannot protect such outputs retroactively.
* '''Nonce introspection keeps its 33-byte result''', within <code>MAX_SCRIPT_ELEMENT_SIZE</code> (520 bytes). Covenants that rebuild an output from it must account for the [[#script-introspection|introspection limits]].
* '''The UTXO set format is unchanged''', because the nonce is not stored in it.
* '''The address type is new and opt-in.''' A wallet that has not upgraded cannot parse or pay an HKE confidential address; existing addresses remain usable.

'''Migration.'''
Moving funds to HKE outputs protects their seeds, but transaction balance can still reveal their amounts.
Wallets must account for the [[#security-and-privacy|migration limits]], including the single-unknown-amount case.
This proposal does not depend on a future post-quantum signature upgrade.

==Test Vectors and Reference Code==

[[elip-hke-confidential-transactions/|<code>elip-hke-confidential-transactions/</code>]] implements key derivation, address encoding and decoding, HKE nonce and placeholder encoding, and sender/recipient seed agreement using the [[elip-0222/|HKE reference implementation]].
[[elip-hke-confidential-transactions/vectors.json|<code>vectors.json</code>]] gives deterministic inputs and expected keys, addresses, ciphertexts, nonces, placeholders and Simplicity nonce hash inputs.
It also lists invalid addresses, one per decoder rejection rule, and an address demonstrating the CRC boundary collision.
The tests also cover malformed encodings, canonical address padding and recipient handling of unusable ciphertexts.
This is reference code, not an Elements consensus, range-proof or Simplicity implementation.

